![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

The current implementation for matching submitted jobs to completed video job sets modifies the `job` objects with temporary properties (`_matchedJobSet`, `_matchMethod`). This can make the data flow harder to follow and requires cleanup (`delete job._matchedJobSet`).

A cleaner approach would be to use a `Map` to store the matches, keyed by `job.sceneIndex`. This avoids mutating the input `job` objects and centralizes the matching results, improving maintainability.

Example of how this could be structured:
```javascript
// At the start of the matching logic
const matches = new Map(); // Map<sceneIndex, { jobSet, method }>

// Strategy 1:
// ... inside loop, when a match is found
if (bestMatch) {
  matches.set(job.sceneIndex, { jobSet: bestMatch, method: 'prompt' });
  matchedJobSetIds.add(bestMatch.id || bestMatch.prompt);
}

// Strategy 2:
// ...
matches.set(job.sceneIndex, { jobSet, method: 'order' });

// Download loop:
for (const job of submittedJobs) {
  if (results.has(job.sceneIndex)) continue;
  const match = matches.get(job.sceneIndex);
  if (!match) continue;

  const { jobSet: bestMatch, method: matchMethod } = match;
  // ... proceed with download
}
```

